{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0124nXTut3F6NiQX5xkPewHV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# tally\n2\t\n3\ttally is a little command-line tool for keeping track of what you spend. It grew\n4\tout of a spreadsheet that got out of hand, and honestly it still carries a bit of\n5\tthat spreadsheet energy — you throw expenses at it, and it adds them up for you by\n6\tmonth and by category. There is no server, no account, no sync. Everything lives\n7\tin a single JSON file next to wherever you run it, which means you can back it up,\n8\tdiff it, or just delete it and start over whenever the mood strikes.\n9\t\n10\tThe idea is that recording an expense should take about as long as it takes to\n11\tthink of it. You type the amount, a category, maybe a note, and you are done. Later,\n12\twhen you want to know where the money went, you ask for a report.\n13\t\n14\t## Installing\n15\t\n16\tThere is nothing to install beyond Python 3.9 or newer. Clone the repository and\n17\trun it as a module:\n18\t\n19\t```\n20\tpython -m tally.cli --help\n21\t```\n22\t\n23\tIf you like, drop a shell alias so you can just type `tally`.\n24\t\n25\t## Recording an expense\n26\t\n27\t```\n28\tpython -m tally.cli add 12.50 food --note \"lunch\"\n29\t```\n30\t\n31\tThe first two arguments are the amount and the category. By default the amount is\n32\tread as US dollars, but you can enter it in another currency and tally will convert\n33\tit for you:\n34\t\n35\t```\n36\tpython -m tally.cli add 39 food --currency EUR --date 2024-03-04\n37\t```\n38\t\n39\tIf you leave off `--date`, today's date is used.\n40\t\n41\t## Looking at your spending\n42\t\n43\tThe `report` command prints category totals for a month. You can narrow it to a\n44\tsingle category too:\n45\t\n46\t```\n47\tpython -m tally.cli report --month 2024-03\n48\tpython -m tally.cli report --month 2024-03 --category food\n49\t```\n50\t\n51\tThere is also a `list` command if you would rather see the individual expenses\n52\tinstead of the totals, and an `export` command that writes everything out as CSV\n53\tso you can open it in a spreadsheet after all:\n54\t\n55\t```\n56\tpython -m tally.cli list --month 2024-03\n57\tpython -m tally.cli export --out march.csv\n58\t```\n59\t\n60\t## How amounts are stored\n61\t\n62\tWhenever you add an expense, tally converts the amount into US dollars using the\n63\tconversion table in `tally/rates.py` and stores that dollar figure. The original\n64\tamount and currency you typed are kept alongside it for reference, but every total\n65\tyou ever see is computed in US dollars. This keeps reports consistent even when a\n66\tmonth mixes several currencies.\n67\t\n68\t## A note on scope\n69\t\n70\tThis is a personal tool, not an accounting package. It does not do tax categories,\n71\treceipts, shared budgets, or anything clever with recurring expenses. If you need\n72\tthose, you have outgrown tally. If you just want to know whether you spent too much\n73\ton takeout last month, you are in the right place.\n74\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b297c7a5-3838-4621-a6f0-d1e9eae72d4e","uuid":"984786e8-1cd7-483b-a73a-32642c59ed92","timestamp":"2026-07-05T16:51:26.003Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sk/.cache/speech-acts-worktrees/rep2/t21-B/README.md","content":"# tally\n\ntally is a little command-line tool for keeping track of what you spend. It grew\nout of a spreadsheet that got out of hand, and honestly it still carries a bit of\nthat spreadsheet energy — you throw expenses at it, and it adds them up for you by\nmonth and by category. There is no server, no account, no sync. Everything lives\nin a single JSON file next to wherever you run it, which means you can back it up,\ndiff it, or just delete it and start over whenever the mood strikes.\n\nThe idea is that recording an expense should take about as long as it takes to\nthink of it. You type the amount, a category, maybe a note, and you are done. Later,\nwhen you want to know where the money went, you ask for a report.\n\n## Installing\n\nThere is nothing to install beyond Python 3.9 or newer. Clone the repository and\nrun it as a module:\n\n```\npython -m tally.cli --help\n```\n\nIf you like, drop a shell alias so you can just type `tally`.\n\n## Recording an expense\n\n```\npython -m tally.cli add 12.50 food --note \"lunch\"\n```\n\nThe first two arguments are the amount and the category. By default the amount is\nread as US dollars, but you can enter it in another currency and tally will convert\nit for you:\n\n```\npython -m tally.cli add 39 food --currency EUR --date 2024-03-04\n```\n\nIf you leave off `--date`, today's date is used.\n\n## Looking at your spending\n\nThe `report` command prints category totals for a month. You can narrow it to a\nsingle category too:\n\n```\npython -m tally.cli report --month 2024-03\npython -m tally.cli report --month 2024-03 --category food\n```\n\nThere is also a `list` command if you would rather see the individual expenses\ninstead of the totals, and an `export` command that writes everything out as CSV\nso you can open it in a spreadsheet after all:\n\n```\npython -m tally.cli list --month 2024-03\npython -m tally.cli export --out march.csv\n```\n\n## How amounts are stored\n\nWhenever you add an expense, tally converts the amount into US dollars using the\nconversion table in `tally/rates.py` and stores that dollar figure. The original\namount and currency you typed are kept alongside it for reference, but every total\nyou ever see is computed in US dollars. This keeps reports consistent even when a\nmonth mixes several currencies.\n\n## A note on scope\n\nThis is a personal tool, not an accounting package. It does not do tax categories,\nreceipts, shared budgets, or anything clever with recurring expenses. If you need\nthose, you have outgrown tally. If you just want to know whether you spent too much\non takeout last month, you are in the right place.\n","numLines":74,"startLine":1,"totalLines":74}}}
